## Why OOP Matters Beyond the Definitions

The four pillars are not just vocabulary for interviews — they are engineering decisions you make every day. The real skill is knowing **when** each pillar applies and when it doesn't.

### Encapsulation: Protecting Invariants, Not Just Hiding Fields

Making fields `private` is the mechanic, but the goal is deeper: you're guaranteeing that the object is **always in a valid state**. Consider a `BankAccount` — if `balance` were public, any code could set it to -500, bypassing business rules. Encapsulation forces all mutations through methods that enforce invariants:

```java
public void withdraw(double amount) {
    if (amount > balance) {
        throw new InsufficientFundsException(
            "Cannot withdraw " + amount + " from balance " + balance
        );
    }
    this.balance -= amount;  // balance can never go negative
}
```

The getters/setters pattern many beginners learn (generate both for every field) actually **weakens** encapsulation. A setter that does nothing but assign defeats the purpose. Only expose what the outside world genuinely needs.

### Inheritance vs Composition: The Modern View

Textbooks teach inheritance as "code reuse." In practice, inheritance creates tight coupling — changing the parent class can silently break every child. The modern guideline is **"prefer composition over inheritance."**

Use inheritance when the relationship is genuinely hierarchical: a `Dog` **is-a** `Animal`, a `SavingsAccount` **is-a** `BankAccount`. Use composition when one class **uses** another: a `Car` **has-a** `Engine`, not **is-a** `Engine`.

```java
// Composition — flexible, testable, swappable
public class OrderService {
    private final PaymentGateway payment;   // interface, injected
    private final NotificationService notifier;

    public OrderService(PaymentGateway payment, NotificationService notifier) {
        this.payment = payment;
        this.notifier = notifier;
    }
}
```

Java enforces single inheritance for classes (`extends` one parent only), which limits the inheritance model. Interfaces with default methods (Java 8+) provide a more flexible alternative for sharing behavior across unrelated types.

### Polymorphism in Real Production Code

The strategy pattern is the most common production use of polymorphism. Instead of `if/else` chains that grow with every new case, you define an interface and swap implementations:

```java
// Without polymorphism — fragile, grows forever
if (paymentType.equals("CREDIT_CARD")) { chargeCreditCard(amount); }
else if (paymentType.equals("PAYPAL")) { chargePaypal(amount); }
else if (paymentType.equals("CRYPTO")) { chargeCrypto(amount); }

// With polymorphism — adding a new type requires zero changes here
public void processPayment(PaymentMethod method, double amount) {
    method.charge(amount);  // runtime dispatch to the correct implementation
}
```

Adding a new payment method means writing one new class that implements `PaymentMethod` — the calling code never changes. This is the **Open/Closed Principle** in action: open for extension, closed for modification.

### Abstraction: The Art of Choosing What to Hide

Abstraction is about **reducing cognitive load**. When you define an interface like `List<T>`, the caller doesn't need to know whether it's backed by an array or linked nodes. They interact with `add()`, `get()`, `remove()` — the "what" — without worrying about the "how."

The practical test: if your interface has 20 methods and the typical user only needs 3, you've abstracted poorly. Good abstractions expose the minimum surface area. This is why Java's `Collection` hierarchy is split into `List`, `Set`, `Queue`, and `Map` rather than one giant interface.

// ENCAPSULATION — private state, public behavior
public class BankAccount {
    private double balance;  // nobody touches this directly

    public void deposit(double amount) {
        if (amount <= 0) throw new IllegalArgumentException("Amount must be positive");
        this.balance += amount;
    }

    public double getBalance() {
        return balance;  // read-only access
    }
}

// INHERITANCE — child reuses parent, adds its own behavior
public class SavingsAccount extends BankAccount {
    private double interestRate;

    public double calculateInterest() {
        return getBalance() * interestRate;
    }
}

// POLYMORPHISM — same method call, different behavior
public void processAccount(BankAccount account) {
    // Works with SavingsAccount, CheckingAccount, or any subclass
    System.out.println("Balance: " + account.getBalance());
}

// ABSTRACTION — hide the "how", expose the "what"
public interface PaymentGateway {
    boolean processPayment(double amount);  // caller doesn't know the implementation
}

// COMPOSITION over INHERITANCE — the modern preference
// Instead of SavingsAccount extends BankAccount,
// use composition when the relationship isn't truly "is-a"

public class NotificationService {
    private final EmailSender emailSender;      // "has-a" email sender
    private final SmsSender smsSender;          // "has-a" SMS sender

    public NotificationService(EmailSender email, SmsSender sms) {
        this.emailSender = email;
        this.smsSender = sms;
    }

    public void notify(User user, String message) {
        if (user.prefersEmail()) {
            emailSender.send(user.getEmail(), message);
        } else {
            smsSender.send(user.getPhone(), message);
        }
    }
}

// RUNTIME POLYMORPHISM in action — strategy pattern
public interface DiscountStrategy {
    double apply(double price);
}

public class PercentageDiscount implements DiscountStrategy {
    private final double percent;
    public PercentageDiscount(double percent) { this.percent = percent; }

    @Override
    public double apply(double price) {
        return price * (1 - percent / 100);  // 20% off → price * 0.8
    }
}

public class FlatDiscount implements DiscountStrategy {
    private final double amount;
    public FlatDiscount(double amount) { this.amount = amount; }

    @Override
    public double apply(double price) {
        return Math.max(0, price - amount);  // subtract flat amount
    }
}

// Usage — caller doesn't know which strategy is used
public double checkout(double price, DiscountStrategy strategy) {
    return strategy.apply(price);  // polymorphic dispatch
}

// Primitives: == works correctly
int x = 5;
int y = 5;
System.out.println(x == y);  // true — values compared directly

// String literals: == works due to String pool
String s1 = "hello";
String s2 = "hello";
System.out.println(s1 == s2);      // true — same pooled object
System.out.println(s1.equals(s2)); // true — same content

// new String(): == fails
String s3 = new String("hello");
String s4 = new String("hello");
System.out.println(s3 == s4);      // false — different heap objects
System.out.println(s3.equals(s4)); // true — same content

// Custom objects: you MUST override equals()
public class Employee {
    private int id;
    private String name;

    @Override
    public boolean equals(Object o) {
        if (this == o) return true;  // same reference = definitely equal
        if (o == null || getClass() != o.getClass()) return false;
        Employee emp = (Employee) o;
        return id == emp.id && Objects.equals(name, emp.name);
    }

    @Override
    public int hashCode() {
        return Objects.hash(id, name);  // MUST override with equals()
    }
}

// INTEGER CACHE TRAP — == works for small values, breaks for large ones
Integer a = 127;
Integer b = 127;
System.out.println(a == b);      // true — cached in IntegerCache[-128..127]

Integer c = 128;
Integer d = 128;
System.out.println(c == d);      // false — new objects, outside cache range
System.out.println(c.equals(d)); // true — always use .equals() for wrappers

// NULL-SAFE COMPARISON with Objects.equals()
String name = null;
// name.equals("Alice");        // NullPointerException!
Objects.equals(name, "Alice");   // false — no exception

// THE equals() CONTRACT (from Object javadoc):
// 1. Reflexive:  a.equals(a) must be true
// 2. Symmetric:  a.equals(b) ↔ b.equals(a)
// 3. Transitive: a.equals(b) && b.equals(c) → a.equals(c)
// 4. Consistent: multiple calls return same result if objects unchanged
// 5. Null:       a.equals(null) must be false

// WHY hashCode() MUST be overridden with equals()
Map<Employee, String> map = new HashMap<>();
Employee emp1 = new Employee(1, "Alice");
map.put(emp1, "Engineering");

Employee emp2 = new Employee(1, "Alice");  // same id and name
System.out.println(emp1.equals(emp2));     // true (we overrode equals)

// Without hashCode override:
System.out.println(map.get(emp2));  // null! Different hash → different bucket

// With hashCode override (Objects.hash(id, name)):
System.out.println(map.get(emp2));  // "Engineering" — correct bucket, equals match

import java.util.*;

// ArrayList — backed by Object[]
List<String> arrayList = new ArrayList<>();
arrayList.add("Alice");   // O(1) amortized — appends to end
arrayList.add("Bob");
arrayList.get(1);          // O(1) — direct index access
arrayList.add(0, "Zara"); // O(n) — shifts all elements right

// LinkedList — backed by doubly-linked nodes
List<String> linkedList = new LinkedList<>();
linkedList.add("Alice");   // O(1) — append to tail
linkedList.addFirst("Zara"); // O(1) — no shifting needed
linkedList.get(1);          // O(n) — must traverse from head

// LinkedList as a Queue (its real strength)
Queue<String> queue = new LinkedList<>();
queue.offer("Task 1");  // enqueue — O(1)
queue.offer("Task 2");
queue.poll();           // dequeue — O(1), returns "Task 1"

// PERFORMANCE COMPARISON — timing real operations
import java.util.*;

// ArrayList: add 100,000 elements, then access by index
List<Integer> arrayList = new ArrayList<>();
for (int i = 0; i < 100_000; i++) arrayList.add(i);  // O(1) amortized each
arrayList.get(50_000);  // O(1) — instant, direct array index

// LinkedList: same 100,000 elements, then access by index
List<Integer> linkedList = new LinkedList<>();
for (int i = 0; i < 100_000; i++) linkedList.add(i);
linkedList.get(50_000);  // O(n) — traverses 50,000 nodes. ~1000x slower

// INSERT AT HEAD — LinkedList's strength
arrayList.add(0, 999);   // O(n) — shifts 100,000 elements right
linkedList.addFirst(999); // O(1) — just update head pointer

// ARRAYDEQUE — better than LinkedList for queue/stack
Deque<String> stack = new ArrayDeque<>();  // prefer over LinkedList
stack.push("first");   // O(1)
stack.push("second");
stack.pop();           // O(1), returns "second"

Queue<String> queue = new ArrayDeque<>();  // prefer over LinkedList
queue.offer("task-1"); // O(1)
queue.offer("task-2");
queue.poll();          // O(1), returns "task-1"

// PRE-SIZING ArrayList when you know the count
List<String> names = new ArrayList<>(10_000);  // avoids ~13 resize operations
// Without pre-sizing: starts at 10, grows 1.5x each time
// 10 → 15 → 22 → 33 → 49 → 73 → 109 → ... → 10,000 (many array copies)

// MEMORY COMPARISON (approximate, 64-bit JVM):
// ArrayList:  ~4 bytes per element (object reference in array)
// LinkedList: ~48 bytes per element (Node object: prev + next + item + overhead)
// That's 12x more memory per element in LinkedList
